course: arts
lesson: 30
title: Dances of the world and dancing at parties
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Almost every people on Earth has its own dances, and most of them are not for showing off but for being together: at weddings, festivals, new years and welcomes.
explain: Many are circle or line dances where everyone links arms or hands and follows the feet, so a beginner can join in after five minutes, the way you join a skipping rhyme in the playground.
explain: Some dances carry deep meaning: the Māori haka is used for welcomes, celebrations, funerals and challenges, and the Japanese Bon Odori honours the spirits of family members from long ago, so they deserve respect.
explain: At a party, nobody is watching you as closely as you think; the uncle doing dad dancing, all elbows and pointing, is usually having the best time in the room.
explain: People say it their own way: "beta" means son or child in Hindi, "mijo" means my son in Spanish, "cringe" means embarrassing, and "chill vibes" means a calm, relaxed feeling.
fact: Bhangra is a lively dance from Punjab with bouncing shoulders and big dhol drums (drums with a skin at each end), popular at weddings in India, Pakistan and the UK.
fact: In the Chinese lion dance two dancers play one lion, moving to drums and cymbals, sometimes jumping on poles; it is said to bring luck.
fact: Many cultures have circle dances, like Greek circle dances, the Jewish hora and the Ukrainian kolomyika, which are about being together more than about skill.
fact: The haka is a Māori ceremonial dance with chanting, stamping and fierce faces; it is used for welcomes, celebrations, funerals and challenges, not only before battles.
fact: The Māori are the first people of New Zealand, and the New Zealand rugby team performs a haka before matches.
fact: Salsa grew up in New York and the Caribbean from Cuban and Puerto Rican music.
fact: Dancers avoid getting dizzy when spinning by spotting: fixing their eyes on one point and whipping the head round to find it again.
fact: Bon Odori is a calm circle dance at Japan's summer Obon festival, which honours the spirits of ancestors.
fact: Dabke is a line dance from the Levant, including Lebanon, Syria, Palestine and Jordan, with linked hands and stamping feet.
fact: Flamenco comes from Andalusia in southern Spain, with Romani roots, and uses guitar, singing, clapping and fast, strong footwork, and it can show proud, angry or sad feelings, not only happy ones.
fact: At parties dancing is about fun, not looking cool, and joining a group is the easiest way in.
exercise: Ravi's aunties pull him in for bhangra at a wedding. Where does bhangra come from? | Punjab | Andalusia, in the south of Spain | The islands of New Zealand | The countryside of northern Japan
exercise: Li Na sees two people inside one lion. What is the lion dance? | A Chinese two-person lion dance | A Spanish dance with a guitar, clapping and stamping feet | A Māori dance of welcome with chanting and fierce faces | A Japanese fan dance
exercise: Why is the lion dance performed at Chinese New Year? | It is said to bring luck | It is a race through the streets to win a big money prize | It celebrates the end of the school year and the start of holidays | Lions are coming
exercise: Nikos tells Callum to "just follow the feet" in a Greek circle dance. What are circle dances mostly about? | Being together | Proving who is the very best dancer at the whole wedding | Winning a prize from the bride and groom at the end of the night | Taking turns to dance alone in the middle while everyone watches
exercise: Maddie thinks the haka is just a war dance. Which is true? | It has many uses, from welcomes to funerals | It is only ever danced just before a battle and at no other time | It is a dance that was invented by a rugby team to scare other teams | It has no meaning
exercise: Who are the Māori? | New Zealand's first people | A famous rugby team from Australia that dances before matches | A group of flamenco dancers from the south of Spain | People of Hawaii
exercise: How do salsa dancers avoid getting dizzy? | Spotting | By closing their eyes tightly every time they start to spin | By spinning only once in the whole night and never again | By eating a big meal before dancing so their tummy stays still
exercise: Where did salsa grow up? | In New York and the Caribbean | In Punjab | In Japan | In Andalusia
exercise: Jacob stands by the wall at the disco. What is likely true? | Most people are busy dancing | Everyone in the hall is staring at him all night long | Dancing at school discos is not really allowed by teachers | Discos are for experts
exercise: Why is Bon Odori calm and simple? | So everyone can join in | Because the dancers are always tired from a long day of work | Because fast dances are banned in Japan during the summer | Babies dance it
exercise: What is the Obon festival about? | Honouring ancestors | Celebrating the new year with fireworks and big parades | A rugby match between two neighbouring towns in summer | Choosing the best dancer in the village to win a prize
exercise: Ahmed's family dances dabke at an Eid party. What is dabke? | A Levant line dance | A Spanish dance with a guitar and lots of loud clapping | A Chinese lion dance with drums, cymbals and poles | A slow Japanese circle dance at a summer festival
exercise: Ivy feels the flamenco stamps in her chest. Where is flamenco from? | Andalusia in southern Spain | Punjab in India | New York in the USA | Kharkiv in Ukraine
exercise: Tom's uncle does dad dancing and everyone cheers. What does this show? | Fun matters more than cool | Dad dancing is against the rules at most weddings in Britain | Uncles must never dance at family weddings, it is tradition | Only kids may dance
exercise: What is a dhol? | A big double-sided drum | A Spanish guitar | A lion costume | A Japanese fan
exercise: Name a Ukrainian circle dance. | Kolomyika | Salsa | Flamenco | Bhangra
test: Your mate goes, "You can't join a wedding dance if you don't know the steps." What is the best answer? | People will show you | You must take dance lessons for at least a year before any wedding | Only members of the family are allowed to dance at a wedding | You have to watch from the side until you know every single step
test: What would happen if you tried to spin ten times fast without spotting? | You would get dizzy | You would stop feeling dizzy after the third spin or so | You would spin faster than ever and feel perfectly fine | Your eyes would fix themselves on a point without you trying
test: A Gen Z kid calls the haka "just a scary dance for TikTok". What is the correction? | It is a Māori ceremony to respect | It was made up for TikTok in 2020 by a group of rugby fans | It has no meaning at all, it is only done for fun before games | It is a Greek dance
test: Your little cousin says the lion dance is "just a costume parade". Which is true? | It takes skill and teamwork | It is a slow parade where nobody inside the costume really moves | A real tame lion is hidden inside the costume for luck | It is for toddlers
test: In a cartoon, everyone at a disco stops and stares at one kid dancing. What is more realistic? | Others are busy dancing | Everyone always stops and watches the shy kid when he dances | Nobody ever dances at discos, they just stand and talk | The music stops each time a new person starts to dance
test: A girl from Glasgow at a Greek wedding says, "I'm pure rubbish at this, so I am." What helps? | Linking arms and following | Leaving the wedding at once before anyone sees her dance | Dancing alone in the middle of the circle to practise | Sitting it out alone
test: Which dance is calm and slow, danced in a circle at a summer festival? | Bon Odori | Flamenco | The lion dance | Bhangra
test: A friend says flamenco "must always look happy, innit". Which is true? | It can show strong or sad feelings | Dances are only allowed to look happy, never sad or angry | Flamenco is a cheerful children's dance with smiles all the way | It is always silent
test: Ravi's auntie says "shoulders up, beta!" ("beta" means child). Which dance is it most likely? | Bhangra, with its bouncing shoulders | Bon Odori, with its slow steps | Flamenco, with its stamping feet | Salsa, with its spins
test: Tom wants the floor to swallow him while his uncle dances. What is a kinder way to see it? | His uncle is having fun | His uncle is breaking the rules of the wedding and should stop | His uncle should be sent home so the young people can dance | Everyone laughs at Tom
test: Which is true about dances like dabke and the hora? | They are danced in lines or circles at celebrations | They are danced alone on a stage | They are only for professionals | They were invented on the internet
exam: Identify the principal technique by which dancers avoid dizziness when executing successive turns. | Spotting: fixing the eyes on one point | The closing of the eyes throughout the entire duration of every turn | The limitation of turning to a single rotation upon each occasion | The consumption of a substantial meal prior to commencing to dance
exam: Which of the following most accurately characterises the haka? | A Māori ceremonial dance of many uses | A dance devised in recent years by a sports team purely for entertainment | A Spanish dance accompanied by guitar, singing and rhythmic clapping | A dance only for battle
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning circle dances in many cultures. | They are principally concerned with community rather than display of skill | They are reserved exclusively for professional dancers | They are performed only by solitary individuals | They are a recent invention of the internet
exam: Ascertain the region from which the dance known as flamenco originates. | Andalusia in southern Spain | Punjab in South Asia | The Caribbean islands | The Levant
